From da19f93e26606de2c7ee14980c8454aa00d96267 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Junod <donujp@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:04:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: Report the connector technology of monitors.

QueryDisplayConfig() always reports an external DisplayPort connection as
the output technology of a monitor, while DisplayConfigGetDeviceInfo() with
DISPLAYCONFIG_DEVICE_INFO_GET_TARGET_NAME always reports an internal one,
for the same monitor. Neither is something Wine knows.

Let the display drivers report what they know. Add struct
wine_monitor_properties in a new wine/monitor.h, carry it in struct
gdi_monitor next to hdr_enabled, store it with the monitor in the registry
next to the HDR property, and report the connector technology from it in
both places. Zero means unknown, and an unknown connector is reported as
DISPLAYCONFIG_OUTPUT_TECHNOLOGY_OTHER, so the value alone is never taken
for HD15, which is also zero. The properties also record where the
information came from (display server, or a monitor Wine made up because
the driver reported none) and whether the driver generated the EDID itself.

winex11.drv reads the RandR ConnectorType property, and marks the EDID it
generates from the preferred mode when the output has none as synthesized,
leaving the Gamescope EDID path alone. winewayland.drv and Xinerama only
set the origin.

Also accept the indirect and DisplayPort USB tunnel output technologies
from drivers, and define the name of the display_device_init mutex in the
new header so that other components can take the lock that win32u holds
while it rebuilds the monitor device nodes.

WINE_GDI_DRIVER_VERSION is incremented because struct gdi_monitor changes.
The user32 tests check that both queries agree with each other and with the
stored properties.
